@startuml
!include style.puml
title App-to-app messaging, across processes

participant "App A\n(worker 0)" as a $usercode
participant "Worker 0" as w0 $worker
participant "Broker" as b $broker
participant "Worker 1" as w1 $worker
participant "App B\n(worker 1)" as bb $usercode

== subscribe, at boot ==
bb -> w1 : watchTopic('house.*')->subscribe(...)
w1 -> b : **Subscribe** { kind: Topic, glob selector }

== publish, at runtime ==
a -> w0 : publish('house.away', [...])
w0 -> b : **Publish** { topic, payload }
b -> b : registry.findRoutes(Topic, 'house.away')
b -> w1 : **TopicMessage** { event, deliverTo }
w1 -> bb : handler(TopicEvent)  <<own fiber>>

note over b
  Unlike state changes, this goes **only** to
  workers holding a matching subscription —
  there is no state cache to keep fresh.
end note

note over a, bb
  Fire-and-forget by design. There is no reply
  channel: an app needing an answer models it as
  a second topic. Request/response would need
  correlation, timeouts, and a deadlock story
  for two apps that ask each other.
end note
@enduml
